mk("Pp88aLB3LpE","How The Pros Build Tension (Without FX Samples)","organic-tension-from-track-sounds",
 "Organic build-up tension without riser samples: one oscillator of the main riff slowly ramps up in pitch, an irregular-length loop of the vocal (or lead) with chorus gets pitch-shifted up over the build, and a fully fed 1/8 delay return is revealed by return-volume automation with rising feedback, all ducked under the dry part",
 "Build tension in a build-up section from material already in the track instead of stock risers: (1) a hidden riser inside the riff synth by ramping one oscillator's pitch upward while the other stays in tune, (2) a sampler loop of the vocal/lead at an irregular, non-tempo length with chorus, faded in and slowly pitch-shifted up, (3) a 100%-wet eighth-note delay fed at full send whose return level and feedback are automated up, then cut cleanly before the drop. Delta vs our riser variants: tension sourced from the track's own parts.",
 ["DAW with automation, return tracks and sidechain","Synth with two oscillators, a sampler with loop points, chorus, a pitch shifter, a tempo-synced delay"],
 [{"name":"build_section","type":"project","description":"The bars leading into a drop/climax, with a riff synth and a vocal or lead part","required":True}],
 [{"name":"tension_layers","type":"audio","description":"Three in-track tension layers automated across the build"}],
 ["Pitch ramps are smooth, monotonic automation over the build (deliberate rises), never random wobble; the riff's other oscillator stays in tune.","Cut delay tails before the drop so it lands cleanly.","Keep feedback below runaway; the echoes should intensify without swamping the mix."],
 [S(1,"Riff riser: on the riff synth, automate only one oscillator's pitch to glide gradually upward across the build while the other oscillator and the notes stay in tune.","Synth osc pitch automation","The riff itself strains upward; no separate riser track"),
  S(2,"Irregular loop: load the vocal (or lead) into a sampler and loop a section whose length is not tied to the tempo; add a wide chorus.","Sampler + chorus","A loop that drifts against the groove"),
  S(3,"Automate that loop's volume and EQ to bring it in over the build; sidechain it lightly to the dry vocal so the main part stays clear.","Volume/EQ automation + sidechain","Subliminal tension that grows"),
  S(4,"Pitch-shift the loop up gradually across the build with automation (a slow, steady upward shift).","Pitch shifter automation","Loop becomes a synthetic riser made from the track"),
  S(5,"Send the vocal group to a return with a 1/8-note delay at 100% wet; set the send to full and leave it; automate the return's fader instead, revealing it only for the swell.","Return track + delay","Wall of echoes controllable by one fader"),
  S(6,"Automate the delay feedback to ramp up slowly through the build (moderate maximum), lightly sidechain the echoes to the dry vocal, and fade the return to zero right before the drop.","Delay feedback automation","Echoes intensify, then stop cleanly at the drop")],
 ["Three tension layers rise over the build and all stop or resolve at the drop.","Only one riff oscillator changes pitch; the riff still reads in key.","No delay tail overlaps the first beat of the drop."],
 ["tension","build-up","riser","arrangement","transition-fx","delay-throw","resampling"])

mk("bAovHufTsc8","How To Make Ambient Pads 100% From Scratch (Vital Tutorial)","staggered-attack-octave-layer-pad",
 "Ambient pad from scratch with staggered layer attacks: sine foundation with light unison, saw and triangle layers on their own filters (triangle an octave up), a separate amp envelope per layer with longer attacks for higher layers, a pitched-down foley texture in the noise slot, slow 4-bar filter LFO, panning LFO, light random on one filter and random level on the foley, then chorus, multiband compression, delay and reverb",
 "Build a lush, developing ambient pad from scratch where each layer arrives at a different time: a sine core is heard first, a saw layer fades in later and an octave-up triangle layer only blooms near the end of each held chord (so held chords get brighter and fuller over time), with a quiet tonal foley texture and slow filter/pan motion. Delta vs our pad variants: per-layer amp envelopes as the main development tool and a foley layer in the noise oscillator.",
 ["Any synth with 3 oscillators, a noise/sample slot, 2 filters, 3+ envelopes, LFOs and random modulators","Chorus, multiband compressor, delay, reverb"],
 [{"name":"chords","type":"midi","description":"Held chord progression","required":True},{"name":"foley_texture","type":"audio","description":"Own recording of a clicky/ambient texture (optional)","required":False}],
 [{"name":"pad","type":"audio","description":"Pad whose upper layers bloom later in each held chord"}],
 ["Steady pitch (Matt's taste): the source also adds an LFO on oscillator pitch bend; leave it off (or tiny) here.","Use your own or free foley recordings; if tonal, pitch it to the track key.","Keep unison light on the sine core."],
 [S(1,"Osc 1: sine with a little unison as the foundation; set amp attack and release moderately long.","Osc 1 + amp env","Soft core"),
  S(2,"Osc 2: saw with unison; Osc 3: triangle with unison, pitched up one octave. Route osc 2 and osc 3 to separate filters.","Oscillators + filter routing","Fuller, layered tone"),
  S(3,"Give each layer its own amp envelope (routed to its level): layer 2 a longer attack, layer 3 a much longer attack so the octave-up layer is mainly heard toward the end of each chord.","Per-layer envelopes","Held chords develop and brighten over time"),
  S(4,"Put a quiet ambient foley texture (e.g. clicks) in the noise slot; pitch it down to the key if it is tonal.","Noise/sample slot","Organic grain under the pad"),
  S(5,"LFO 1: triangle reshaped to open slowly and close quickly, tempo 4 bars (4/1), on one filter's cutoff. LFO 2: sine, bipolar, on osc 3 pan.","LFOs","Slow opening sweeps and gentle stereo motion"),
  S(6,"Add a small random modulation on the saw layer's filter and a random modulator on the foley level so it comes and goes.","Random modulators","No two chords sound identical"),
  S(7,"FX: a bit of chorus to tie oscillators together, multiband compression to bring up subtle detail, then delay and reverb.","FX chain","Lush finished pad")],
 ["The octave-up layer is clearly louder at the end of a held chord than at its start.","Filter sweep completes about once every 4 bars.","No pitch modulation is audible."],
 ["pad","ambient","layering","envelopes","octave-layer","brightness","foley"])

mk("9Re-L7z0068","How to SYNTH DRONE without boring everyone","drone-role-pedal-point-and-polychords",
 "Make drones interesting by role: a bass pedal point under changing harmony (or a repeated arpeggiated root), a non-key-tracked mid-range drone as a through-line with the bass and an upper note moving around it, layered drones tuned to a static chord with a progression underneath for polychords, and wavetable-position sweeps for texture",
 "Decide what a drone does in the track and write around it: as a pedal point (one bass note held or repeated for the whole track while chords and textures change above), as a mid-range 'connective tissue' oscillator (pitch tracking off) that the bass line and a note above it rub against, or as a stack of drones forming a fixed chord over a moving progression to create polychords and unresolved tension. Keep exposed drones alive with slow wavetable-position sweeps.",
 ["Synth where one oscillator can have key tracking off (fixed pitch), or a separate drone voice","Wavetable synth with a modulatable position","Basic chord knowledge"],
 [{"name":"key","type":"string","description":"Track key and the drone note(s)","required":True}],
 [{"name":"drone_arrangement","type":"midi+audio","description":"Drone layer plus the moving parts written around it"}],
 ["Steady pitch (Matt's taste): the source likes wobbly tape loops on drones; skip tape wow/flutter or keep it tiny and never on the foundation.","A drone used as the foundation locks the low end; then interest must come from harmony, texture and rhythm above it.","Do not stack several drones in the low end; keep stacked drones in the mids/high mids."],
 [S(1,"Choose the drone's role: foundation (bass pedal point) or textural through-line in the mids/high mids.","Planning","Role decided"),
  S(2,"Pedal point: hold or repeat one bass note for the whole piece (an arpeggiator repeating the root counts); change chords, textures and rhythm above it, e.g. hold the dominant to build tension before resolving to the tonic.","Bass voice + chords","Tension from harmony moving over a fixed bass"),
  S(3,"Through-line: switch off key tracking on one oscillator and tune it to a mid-range note; play bass notes and a note above that move around it, auditioning chords for the rubs they create. A second fixed partial (e.g. A440) can be added rhythmically.","Synth with fixed-pitch oscillator","A constant middle tone with harmony shifting around it; bass line free to move"),
  S(4,"Polychords: tune several mid/high drones to a static chord and play a different chord progression underneath so the two combine into polychords that never fully resolve.","Drone stack + progression","Uneasy, angular harmony"),
  S(5,"Texture: on exposed drones, find sweet spots in a rich wavetable and sweep the position slowly with an LFO.","Wavetable + slow LFO","Evolving timbre on a static pitch"),
  S(6,"Change the bass note under the drone at key moments; a single new pedal note recolours everything above it.","Arrangement","Big harmonic shifts from one note")],
 ["The drone pitch never changes; harmonic interest comes from parts around it.","At least one moment where a new bass note recolours the drone.","Wavetable position moves slowly on exposed drones."],
 ["drone","pedal-point","harmony","polychords","ambient","wavetable","music-theory"])

mk("Dscd9Q79fwQ","Build tension for your action cue: Metric modulation, time signature changes, ostinatos and more !","metric-modulation-action-cue",
 "Action-cue tension with metric modulation: tempo map from sync-point markers, 100 BPM 4/4 to quarter-note triplet = 150 BPM, 3/4 -> 6/8 -> 5/8 (truncate an eighth), dotted-eighth = 133 BPM, return to tempo primo; piano sketch with ostinatos from one motif, then an edited MIDI rhythm track and orchestration by role",
 "Turn one simple motif into a minute of escalating action music by changing tempo and meter smoothly instead of randomly: mark sync points, then use metric modulation (a subdivision of the old beat becomes the new beat) plus time-signature changes to hit them, sketch on piano with ostinatos and motif variations, build a temp rhythm track from loop MIDI that you edit (accents, rests), and orchestrate with low ostinatos, supporting winds/brass and a percussive piano.",
 ["DAW with a tempo/signature track and markers","Piano and any orchestral or synth ensemble sounds; a rhythm-loop instrument that can export MIDI"],
 [{"name":"motif","type":"midi","description":"A short simple piano motif","required":True},{"name":"sync_points","type":"list","description":"Times (s or SMPTE) the music must hit","required":False}],
 [{"name":"cue","type":"midi+audio","description":"~1-minute cue with a tempo map of metric modulations hitting the sync points"}],
 ["Tempo changes follow metric relationships (new tempo = old tempo x subdivision ratio), not arbitrary jumps.","Edit loop MIDI (accents, rests, notes) so the rhythm track is not a stock loop.","Synth voices keep steady pitch (no random wobble)."],
 [S(1,"Set a starting tempo and meter (e.g. 100 BPM, 4/4) and place markers at each sync point.","Tempo track + markers","Grid of hit points"),
  S(2,"Metric modulation up: let the quarter-note triplet of the old tempo become the new beat (100 x 3/2 = 150 BPM).","Tempo track","Smooth, faster feel"),
  S(3,"Change meters to fit sync points and add drive: 3/4 -> 6/8, then 5/8 by truncating one eighth note.","Signature track","Off-balance, urgent phrasing"),
  S(4,"Modulate again: make the dotted eighth of the old tempo the new quarter (100 / 0.75 = ~133 BPM); finally return to the opening tempo (tempo primo) to end the cycle.","Tempo track","Escalation then return"),
  S(5,"Piano sketch over the tempo map: derive ostinatos and simple variations from the motif.","Piano","Full-length sketch from one idea"),
  S(6,"Temp rhythm track: export loop MIDI into the DAW and edit accents, rests and notes so it fits your sections.","Loop instrument + MIDI editor","Personal, non-prefab rhythm bed"),
  S(7,"Orchestrate by role: replace the piano opening with a harp-like plucked voice, double low ostinatos in low strings/bass synth, use winds to support and brass (the loudest section) for key dynamic points; bring piano back as a percussive ostinato; add clusters/effects at the opening.","Instrument assignment","Escalating, coherent cue")],
 ["Every tempo change can be written as old BPM x ratio (3/2, 4/3...).","Section boundaries line up with the sync-point markers.","The motif is recognisable in at least two variations."],
 ["arrangement","tension","metric-modulation","odd-meter","ostinato","cinematic","music-theory","rhythm"])

mk("Fj7E0DXML1o","Drum programming Tips for ANY machine","velocity-and-flam-groove-on-step-sequencer",
 "Human drums on any step sequencer: four-on-the-floor kick, eighth hats with the hats that land on the kick turned down, 56% swing, a 16th-hat layer only on the second bar with every other hit softer, open hat on the last step of bar 2, a loose clap built from 3-4 stacked clap tracks nudged early/late with varied velocity and pitch, a track with an odd 24-step second bar and an 8-bar reset, random sample-start drift on the closed hat, and a pumping bus compressor",
 "Get movement and feel into rigid step-sequenced drums with velocity, micro-timing and pattern length rather than more parts: accent the off-beat hats, add a second-bar 16th hat layer, build a loose 'flammed' clap from stacked copies shifted slightly early and late, let one track's pattern length make a cycle against the bar, and glue with a pumping compressor. Delta vs our groove variants: four-on-the-floor velocity shaping, stacked-clap flam and odd-length pattern resets.",
 ["Any step sequencer or drum machine with per-step velocity, swing, micro-timing, per-track length and an LFO","Kick, closed/open hat and clap sounds (synthesized or own samples)"],
 [{"name":"tempo","type":"number","description":"Track tempo","required":True}],
 [{"name":"groove","type":"midi+audio","description":"2-bar (and 8-bar cycle) drum groove with human feel"}],
 ["Keep the kick on the grid; feel comes from hats and claps.","Micro-timing offsets are small and fixed per copy (no random pitch).","Bring the drums in early (by about count 8)."],
 [S(1,"Program a four-on-the-floor kick.","Sequencer","Steady pulse"),
  S(2,"Eighth hats: lower the velocity of the hats that coincide with the kick so the off-beat hats stand out; set swing to about 56%.","Velocity + swing","Bouncy hat line that sounds compressed"),
  S(3,"On a second hat track, add 16th hats only in the second bar of a two-bar pattern, with every other hit lower in velocity.","Second hat track (32 steps)","Bar-2 lift"),
  S(4,"Put a softer, slightly pitched-down open hat on step 15 of bar 2 with a little reverb send.","Open hat track","Phrase-end lift"),
  S(5,"Loose clap: copy the clap to 3-4 tracks; leave one on the grid, push one a little early, one later and one even later; vary velocities and pitch one copy up (and one far down, quieter, high-passed); add a little reverb.","Stacked clap tracks + micro-timing","A wide, loose, flammed clap"),
  S(6,"Make one track 32 steps with the second bar shortened to 24 steps and a pattern reset every 8 bars (128 steps), so it cycles against the bar.","Per-track length + reset","Variation over an 8-bar cycle"),
  S(7,"On the closed hat, route a slow, free LFO (odd length) at low depth to sample start, with a soft attack.","LFO -> sample start","Subtle hit-to-hit timbre drift"),
  S(8,"Balance the claps down, then add a pumping bus compressor; if the kick blooms too much, shorten it and tune it down slightly.","Bus compressor","Glued, pumping groove")],
 ["Kick-aligned hats are measurably lower in velocity than off-beat hats.","Clap copies are offset by small, different amounts.","Pattern repeats exactly only every 8 bars."],
 ["drums","groove","velocity","swing","micro-timing","house","step-sequencer"])

mk("GomiGC__HC0","Saturation Is Killing Your Mix","dynamics-restoring-saturation",
 "Saturate drums without losing punch: an envelope follower reads the clean signal on a muted parallel chain, and its output drives a gain stage placed after the saturator so the saturated signal's peaks are pushed back up and its dynamic range is restored",
 "Add saturation warmth to drums (or a melody loop) while keeping their punch: because saturation shaves peaks and flattens dynamic range, measure the dry signal's envelope on a muted parallel chain and use it to modulate a gain stage after the saturator, so loud hits get louder again. Delta vs our saturation variants: envelope-follower-driven gain restoration after the saturator.",
 ["DAW with an envelope follower / peak controller that can modulate a parameter","Any saturator and a gain/utility device"],
 [{"name":"source","type":"audio","description":"Drum bus or loop that loses punch when saturated","required":True}],
 [{"name":"punchy_saturated","type":"audio","description":"Saturated signal with restored transients"}],
 ["Only gain is modulated after the saturator; nothing else in that chain.","Values (base/depth) are set by ear; the source quotes about 30-40% base and 60-70% amount on its follower.","Original routing recipe; the free patch offered in the source is not needed."],
 [S(1,"Saturate the drums and watch the waveform: peaks get shaved and the drums stop hitting in the mix.","Saturator + scope","Problem confirmed: warmth but lost punch"),
  S(2,"Build two parallel chains: chain A holds only an envelope follower/peak controller and is muted (it just measures); chain B holds the saturator followed by a gain/utility device.","Rack / parallel chains","Measuring chain + processing chain"),
  S(3,"Map the follower's output to the gain device in chain B.","Modulation mapping","Gain rises with each dry peak"),
  S(4,"Set the follower base around 30-40% and the amount around 60-70%, then adjust until saturated hits match the original punch.","Follower settings","Warmth kept, peaks restored"),
  S(5,"Compare in the mix; for melodic loops combine with sidechain ducking to the drum loop for pump.","A/B","Punchy drums with saturation colour")],
 ["Peak-to-RMS (crest factor) of the processed drums is close to the dry drums while harmonics are added.","Chain A passes no audio."],
 ["saturation","drums","punch","dynamics","envelope-follower","mixing","punchy-bass"])

mk("PE3c8J6glho","Turn an Ambient Loop into a Full Track in Bitwig Studio (Part 2)","ambient-loop-to-track-arp-rate-energy",
 "Ambient loop to full track: split the chord progression into single-chord clips, intro from the loop, slower evolving section -> 'chorus' with faster chord changes -> slow outro; per-layer slow movement (band-pass pad filter with two LFOs, random LP and comb on the atmos, slow auto-pan), cutoff 'spikes' on the bass drone, arpeggiator rate automated whole -> half -> quarter -> eighth notes for energy, a double-speed drum clip and an arpeggiated pluck bass replacing the drone in the peak",
 "Expand a finished ambient loop into a structured track by turning it into rearrangeable single-chord clips and using rhythmic density as the energy control: the arp's note rate steps up from whole notes to eighths as sections intensify, the chorus changes chords more often, drums go double-time by scaling the clip to 50%, and an arpeggiated pluck bass takes over from the drone at the peak. Delta vs our loop-to-song variants: arp-rate and harmonic-rhythm as the section energy dials.",
 ["DAW with clip launcher or loop regions, automation and clip time-scaling","Synth with arpeggiator, filters with modulators, comb filter, auto-pan/utility"],
 [{"name":"loop","type":"project","description":"Finished ambient loop: pad, atmos, bass drone, arp pluck, drum clip","required":True}],
 [{"name":"arrangement","type":"project","description":"Intro, evolving section, chorus-like peak, outro"}],
 ["Drums early (Matt's taste) overrides the source's slow start: bring the drum clip in by about count 8 even in the evolving section; keep the double-time clip for the peak.","Modulators move filters/pan only, not pitch.","Mute the drone while the pluck bass plays so they do not compete."],
 [S(1,"Duplicate the loop to a clip slot and cut the chord progression into single-chord clips (consolidate each first).","Clip launcher / editor","Rearrangeable chord blocks"),
  S(2,"Make a 16-bar intro from the loop scene.","Timeline","Intro section"),
  S(3,"Pad: band-pass (2-pole) filter with two independent slow LFOs on frequency and resonance. Atmos: smooth slow random modulator (unipolar, subtle) on a low-pass cutoff, a comb filter with a slow LFO on feedback and smooth random on frequency, and a very slow bipolar LFO on pan.","Filters + modulators","Each layer moves slowly on its own"),
  S(4,"Automate the bass drone's oscillator cutoff in short 'spikes', copy the automation along the timeline and vary level/shape.","Automation","Periodic flares in the drone"),
  S(5,"Arrange: slower evolving section, then a chorus-like section with more frequent chord changes and higher dynamics, then a slower outro.","Timeline","Clear energy arc"),
  S(6,"Automate the arpeggiator rate: whole notes in the slow section, then half, quarter and eighth notes as energy rises (eighths in the chorus).","Arp rate automation","Rhythmic density tracks the arc"),
  S(7,"Duplicate the drum clip and scale it to 50% length for a double-time peak section; sprinkle squelch hits, moving them by octaves for faster/slower squelches.","Clip scaling","Faster drums at the peak"),
  S(8,"For the peak, add an arpeggiated pluck bass fed with the pad chords (tune its ratio/octave lower for depth) and mute the bass drone there; finish with lightly EQ'd, delayed, reverbed one-shots.","Synth + arpeggiator","Peak section with a moving bass")],
 ["Arp rate automation increases step-wise toward the peak and drops in the outro.","Peak section has the double-time drum clip and no drone/pluck-bass overlap.","Drums enter by about 10-12 s."],
 ["arrangement","loop-to-song","ambient","arpeggiator","energy-curve","sections","loops"])
